  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Austin building codes are the city's adopted versions of the International Code Council model codes plus local amendments, and they govern everything from your foundation to your attic insulation. If you are building a new home inside city limits, you will deal with them through the <strong>City of Austin Development Services Department (DSD)</strong>. Here is how the codes are organized, where Austin differs, and how to stay compliant from plan review to final inspection.</p>

<h2>Why codes matter more than paperwork</h2>
<p>It is easy to think of codes as hoops. In practice they are a minimum standard written from decades of failures: houses that burned, roofs that blew off, foundations that cracked. Meeting code protects your family, keeps your insurance and financing on track, and makes resale much simpler. Skipping it can mean stop-work orders, fines, or tearing out finished work so an inspector can see what is behind it.</p>

<h2>The codes that apply to an Austin home</h2>
<p>Austin, like most Texas cities, adopts model codes and amends them. For a new house, the main ones are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>International Residential Code (IRC):</strong> the core code for one- and two-family homes and townhouses, covering foundations, framing, roofing, egress, stairs and more.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical code:</strong> based on the National Electrical Code (NEC), covering wiring, circuits, grounding and protection devices.</li>
<li><strong>Plumbing code:</strong> Austin has historically used the Uniform Plumbing Code (UPC) rather than the IRC plumbing chapters. Confirm what is current.</li>
<li><strong>Mechanical code:</strong> HVAC equipment, ductwork and ventilation.</li>
<li><strong>Energy code:</strong> based on the International Energy Conservation Code (IECC) with local amendments.</li>
<li><strong>Fire code:</strong> mostly a commercial concern, but it affects things like address visibility and access.</li>
</ul>
<p>The International Building Code (IBC) applies to commercial and larger multifamily buildings, not typical single-family homes. Austin updates its adopted editions periodically, so check the City of Austin's technical codes information on the <a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/department/development-services">Development Services Department</a> site before you finalize plans.</p>

<h2>Where Austin goes beyond the model codes</h2>
<h3>Energy and green building</h3>
<p>Austin has long pushed energy performance beyond state minimums. Expect plan reviewers and inspectors to look closely at insulation, air sealing, window performance, duct sealing and equipment efficiency. The <strong>Austin Energy Green Building</strong> program offers ratings for homes that go further on energy, water and materials, and some projects are required to participate. Even if yours is not, its guidelines are a useful benchmark. Our guide to <a href="/energy-efficient-home-building-in-the-texas-heat/">energy-efficient home building in the Texas heat</a> covers practical choices.</p>
<h3>Trees, watersheds and impervious cover</h3>
<p>Austin's land development rules protect larger trees, limit impervious cover and add requirements in sensitive watersheds. These are not building code in the strict sense, but they shape your site plan and can change your footprint. Get your tree survey and site constraints in hand before design.</p>
<h3>Code interpretations</h3>
<p>Model codes leave room for judgment. The city publishes interpretations explaining how certain provisions are applied locally. When a reviewer comment does not make sense, search those interpretations first, then ask.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask your designer to list the adopted code editions and key local amendments right on the cover sheet of your plans. Reviewers appreciate it, and it forces everyone on the team to work from the same rulebook.</p></div>

<h2>Permits: when you need one</h2>
<p>For a new home, you need a building permit plus separate trade permits. Generally, permits are required for:</p>
<ul>
<li>New construction and additions</li>
<li>Structural changes and major remodels</li>
<li>Electrical, plumbing and mechanical work</li>
<li>Many decks, fences over certain heights, pools and driveways</li>
</ul>
<p>Trade permits are typically pulled by state-licensed electricians, plumbers and HVAC contractors. Fees and review times change, so look them up when you are ready to apply. For the full sequence from zoning to utilities, see <a href="/requirements-when-building-a-new-house-in-austin-texas/">requirements when building a new house in Austin</a>.</p>

<h2>Inspections you should expect</h2>
<ol>
<li>Plumbing rough-in under the slab</li>
<li>Foundation (pre-pour)</li>
<li>Framing with rough electrical, plumbing and mechanical</li>
<li>Insulation and energy checks, which may include blower door and duct leakage testing</li>
<li>Final building and trade inspections</li>
</ol>
<p>Schedule inspections promptly and never cover work that has not passed. A failed item is a normal part of building; just fix it completely before reinspection. A good illustrated reference such as [[amz:books_code_check|Code Check]] helps you pre-inspect your own job. A [[amz:moisture_meter|moisture meter]] is handy too, since framing that is too wet before drywall causes problems code does not always catch.</p>
<!--products:books_code_check,books_code_check_electrical,moisture_meter|Tools for pre-checking your work before the inspector arrives-->

<h2>How to stay compliant and avoid common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Research before you design.</strong> Confirm zoning, site rules and code editions first.</li>
<li><strong>Hire licensed trades.</strong> Electrical, plumbing and HVAC must be done and permitted by state-licensed pros.</li>
<li><strong>Keep plans and field work matched.</strong> Changes in the field may need a plan revision.</li>
<li><strong>Document everything.</strong> Keep inspection results, photos before cover-up and engineer letters in one binder.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for updates.</strong> Austin revises codes and land development rules; check before each new project.</li>
</ul>
<p>Comparing Austin with other metros? Read our overview of <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">Texas building codes</a>.</p>

<h2>What changes most often between code editions</h2>
<p>When Austin moves to a newer code edition, the changes that affect homeowners most tend to fall into a few areas: energy requirements (insulation, air leakage and duct testing), electrical protection requirements such as AFCI and GFCI coverage, smoke and carbon monoxide alarm rules, and stair, guard and egress details. If your designer is reusing plans drawn a few years ago, ask them to review those items specifically against the current edition. Plans that were fine under an older edition can draw a round of reviewer comments under a newer one.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Bookmark the City of Austin Development Services Department pages, confirm the current code editions and fees, and get your site surveys started. If you are planning to run the build yourself, our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> walks through permits, inspections and scheduling step by step.</p>
